1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,983
VMware operations teams handle inventory checks, incident investigations and repeatable changes every day.

2
00:00:06,983 --> 00:00:11,727
VMware Skills brings AI assistance and focused automation to that work.

3
00:00:11,727 --> 00:00:20,752
It is designed to reduce repetitive information gathering while keeping evidence, existing permissions and auditability in the workflow.

4
00:00:20,752 --> 00:00:22,992
Start with the read-only Monitor.

5
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:29,738
The family covers vSphere, networking, storage, observability and more.

6
00:00:29,738 --> 00:00:31,918
Pilot coordinates workflows.

7
00:00:31,918 --> 00:00:35,827
Domain servers connect to their management systems.

8
00:00:35,827 --> 00:00:40,564
Policy provides shared checks, sanitization and audit logging.

9
00:00:40,564 --> 00:00:43,120
Start with the read-only Monitor.

10
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:48,273
Here is a historical screenshot from an actual session.

11
00:00:48,273 --> 00:00:53,781
The inventory view returned one vCenter, two standalone hosts, and thirteen VMs.

12
00:00:53,781 --> 00:00:58,133
The notes column containing environment identifiers is omitted.

13
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:02,893
These are recorded results, not a fresh inventory of the environment.

14
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:09,280
The health screenshot records connected hosts and critical alarms in the same view.

15
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:11,920
That does not establish a current incident.

16
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:18,940
Check when the observation was captured and whether the alarms still apply before deciding what to investigate next.

17
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:25,738
When changes are needed, use the relevant domain skill and a reviewed workflow.

18
00:01:25,738 --> 00:01:29,681
Existing VMware permissions and local policy still apply.

19
00:01:29,681 --> 00:01:33,352
Destructive tools use preview and confirmation gates.

20
00:01:33,352 --> 00:01:39,063
A confirmation flag is not independent human approval, and rollback is best effort.

21
00:01:40,467 --> 00:01:45,507
An optional model self-test helps your team assess which model fits its tasks.

22
00:01:45,507 --> 00:01:50,548
Compare the same synthetic scenarios using automatic checks and human review.

23
00:01:50,548 --> 00:01:57,075
This supports model choice, without executing VMware changes or guaranteeing production reliability.

24
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:00,799
Finally, follow the audit trail.

25
00:02:00,799 --> 00:02:09,347
This is an assistant-prepared summary of exported records, showing time, skill, tool, MCP or CLI, status and duration.

26
00:02:09,347 --> 00:02:12,938
We omit the target column and show fourteen rows.

27
00:02:12,938 --> 00:02:18,972
Names reflect the captured version; use the original records to check completeness.

28
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:28,593
The goal is practical: less repetitive work, clearer investigations and traceable operational workflows for VMware teams.

29
00:02:28,593 --> 00:02:34,483
Start with one read-only task, validate the result, and add automation where it helps.

30
00:02:34,483 --> 00:02:40,036
VMware Skills is a community project, not an official VMware or Broadcom product.

31
00:02:40,036 --> 00:02:42,744
Feedback and contributions are welcome.
